insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$bell-s-palsy$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1219$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Bell's Palsy$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$nervous$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Bell's Palsy", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Acute unilateral facial paralysis with a clear presentation; key teaching is distinguishing it from stroke and other causes of facial weakness by exam.", "kind": "disease", "category": "cranial neuropathy", "organ_systems": ["nervous"], "primary_clerkship": "neurology", "secondary_clerkships": ["emergency", "familyMedicine", "internalMedicine"], "commonness": "common", "settings": ["clinic", "emergency"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Bell's palsy is the most common cause of facial paralysis. It usually affects just one side of the face. Symptoms appear suddenly and are at their worst about 48 hours after they start. They can range from mild to severe and include:
- Twitching
- Weakness
- Paralysis
- Drooping eyelid or corner of mouth
- Drooling
- Dry eye or mouth
- Excessive tearing in the eye
- Impaired ability to taste
Scientists think that a viral infection makes the facial nerve swell or become inflamed. You are most likely to get Bell's palsy if you are pregnant, diabetic or sick with a cold or flu.
Three out of four patients improve without treatment. With or without treatment, most people begin to get better within 2 weeks and recover completely within 3 to 6 months.
NIH: National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke
MeSH definition (Bell Palsy): A syndrome characterized by the acute onset of unilateral FACIAL PARALYSIS which progresses over a 2-5 day period. Weakness of the orbicularis oculi muscle and resulting incomplete eye closure may be associated with corneal injury. Pain behind the ear often precedes the onset of paralysis. This condition may be associated with HERPESVIRUS 1, HUMAN infection of the facial nerve. (Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, p1376)
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Behcet's Syndrome, Cytomegalovirus Infections, Herpes Simplex, Kaposi Sarcoma, Oral Cancer, Salivary Gland Disorders, Tongue Disorders$rounds_kb$)
