insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$brachial-plexus-injuries$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-6089$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Brachial Plexus Injuries$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$uncommon$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$surgery$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$nervous$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$musculoskeletal$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Brachial Plexus Injuries", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Focal neurologic deficit in the upper extremity reachable by history, exam and imaging/EMG; includes obstetric and traumatic forms with clear localization teaching.", "kind": "injury", "category": "peripheral nerve injury", "organ_systems": ["nervous", "musculoskeletal"], "primary_clerkship": "neurology", "secondary_clerkships": ["emergency", "pediatrics", "surgery"], "commonness": "uncommon", "settings": ["emergency", "clinic", "inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$The brachial plexus is a network of nerves that sends signals from the spine to the shoulder, arm, and hand. Damage to the brachial plexus can cause symptoms such as:
- A limp or paralyzed arm
- Lack of muscle control in the arm, hand, or wrist
- Lack of feeling or sensation in the arm or hand
Brachial plexus injuries can happen because of shoulder trauma, tumors, or inflammation. Sometimes they happen during childbirth when a baby's shoulders become stuck during delivery and the nerves stretch or tear.
Some brachial plexus injuries may heal without treatment. Many children who are injured during birth improve or recover by 3 to 4 months of age. Treatment includes physical therapy and, in some cases, surgery.
NIH: National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke
MeSH definition (Brachial Plexus Neuropathies): Diseases of the cervical (and first thoracic) roots, nerve trunks, cords, and peripheral nerve components of the BRACHIAL PLEXUS. Clinical manifestations include regional pain, PARESTHESIA; MUSCLE WEAKNESS, and decreased sensation (HYPESTHESIA) in the upper extremity. These disorders may be associated with trauma (including BIRTH INJURIES); THORACIC OUTLET SYNDROME; NEOPLASMS; NEURITIS; RADIOTHERAPY; and other conditions. (From Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, pp1351-2)
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Complex Regional Pain Syndrome, Diabetic Nerve Problems, Peripheral Nerve Disorders$rounds_kb$)
