insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$child-mental-health$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-3162$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Child Mental Health$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$psychiatry$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$psychiatry$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$behavioral$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Child Mental Health", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "Umbrella term for pediatric psychiatric illness, not a specific diagnosis a student can reach by interview and workup.", "kind": "other", "category": "psychiatric category", "organ_systems": ["behavioral"], "primary_clerkship": "psychiatry", "secondary_clerkships": ["pediatrics"], "commonness": "common", "settings": ["psychiatric", "pediatric_clinic"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$It's important to recognize and treat mental illnesses in children early on. Once mental illness develops, it becomes a regular part of your child's behavior. This makes it more difficult to treat.
But it's not always easy to know when your child has a serious problem. Everyday stresses can cause changes in your child's behavior. For example, getting a new brother or sister or going to a new school may cause a child to temporarily act out. Warning signs that it might be a more serious problem include:
- Problems in more than one setting (at school, at home, with peers)
- Changes in appetite or sleep
- Social withdrawal or fear of things he or she did not used to be not afraid of
- Returning to behaviors more common in younger children, such as bedwetting
- Signs of being upset, such as sadness or tearfulness
- Signs of self-destructive behavior, such as head-banging or suddenly getting hurt often
- Repeated thoughts of death
To diagnose mental health problems, the doctor or mental health specialist looks at your child's signs and symptoms, medical history, and family history. Treatments include medicines and talk therapy.
NIH: National Institute of Mental Health
MeSH definition (Mental Disorders): Psychiatric illness or diseases manifested by breakdowns in the adaptational process expressed primarily as abnormalities of thought, feeling, and behavior producing either distress or impairment of function.
MeSH definition (Mental Health): Emotional, psychological, and social well-being of an individual or group.
MeSH definition (Psychology, Child): Branch of psychology involving the study of normal and abnormal behavior of children.
MeSH definition (Child Psychiatry): The medical science that deals with the origin, diagnosis, prevention, and treatment of mental disorders in children.
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Anxiety, Drug Use and Addiction, Drugs and Young People, Eating Disorders, Mental Disorders, Mood Disorders, Personality Disorders, Pregnancy and Substance Use, Prescription Drug Misuse, Sexual Problems in Women, Sleep Disorders, Veterans and Military Health$rounds_kb$)
