insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$diabetes-type-1$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1339$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Diabetes Type 1$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$endocrine$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Diabetes Type 1", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Classic presentation with polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss and possible DKA; diagnosis reachable by history, labs and ketone assessment.", "kind": "disease", "category": "endocrine/metabolic", "organ_systems": ["endocrine"], "primary_clerkship": "pediatrics", "secondary_clerkships": ["internalMedicine", "emergency", "familyMedicine"], "commonness": "common", "settings": ["emergency", "inpatient", "pediatric_clinic", "clinic"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$What is type 1 diabetes?
Diabetes means your blood glucose, also called blood sugar, is too high. With type 1 diabetes, your body makes little or no insulin. Insulin is a hormone from your pancreas that helps glucose move from your body into your cells, where it is used for energy. Your body can make glucose, but it also comes from the food you eat. Without insulin, too much glucose stays in your blood.
Over time, high blood glucose can lead to serious problems with your heart, eyes, kidneys, nerves, gums, and teeth.
If your blood glucose gets very high, you can develop diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). It happens when your body doesn't have enough insulin to allow glucose into your cells. Instead, your liver breaks down fat for energy, which produces ketones. When ketones build up too quickly, they can reach dangerous levels. DKA is a life-threatening emergency.
What causes type 1 diabetes?
Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disease. Your immune system mistakenly attacks the cells in your pancreas that make insulin. Researchers don't yet know exactly why this happens. Genes and environmental factors may play a role in triggering the disease.
Who is more likely to develop type 1 diabetes?
Type 1 diabetes often begins in children and young adults, but it can appear at any age. Having a parent or sibling with type 1 diabetes may increase your chance of developing it.
What are the symptoms of type 1 diabetes?
Symptoms often start quickly, especially in children. In adults, they may develop more slowly. Symptoms may include:
- Feeling very thirsty
- Feeling very hungry
- Urinating (peeing) more often, including at night
- Having fatigue
- Having blurry vision
- Losing feeling in your feet, or having tingling in your feet or hands
- Having sores that don't heal
- Losing weight without trying
How is type 1 diabetes diagnosed?
Blood tests that measure your blood glucose level can show whether it is higher than normal for you.
What are the treatments for type 1 diabetes?
Type 1 diabetes is a chronic (long-term) condition. You will need daily insulin, either through injections or an insulin pump. Some people also take another diabetes medicine that works with insulin.
Ask your health care provider about the best way to check your blood glucose level and how often you should check it.
Can type 1 diabetes be prevented?
Type 1 diabetes can't be prevented. But you may be able to prevent or delay health problems related to diabetes by:
- Managing your blood glucose level with finger prick tests or a continuous glucose monitor (CGM)
- Making healthy food choices
- Getting regular exercise
A blood test called the A1C can show how well you are managing your diabetes over time.
NIH: National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases
MeSH definition (Diabetes Mellitus, Type 1): A subtype of DIABETES MELLITUS that is characterized by INSULIN deficiency. It is manifested by the sudden onset of severe HYPERGLYCEMIA, rapid progression to DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS, and DEATH unless treated with insulin. The disease may occur at any age, but is most common in childhood or adolescence.
Also called: Insulin-dependent diabetes, Juvenile diabetes, Type I diabetes
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Addison Disease, Diabetes Complications, Diabetes Type 2, How to Prevent Diabetes, Juvenile Arthritis, Lupus, Pemphigus, Prediabetes, Rheumatoid Arthritis$rounds_kb$)
