insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$dwarfism$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1244$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Dwarfism$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$uncommon$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$musculoskeletal$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$endocrine$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Dwarfism", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "A descriptive category of short stature encompassing many distinct conditions rather than a single diagnosable disease.", "kind": "other", "category": "growth disorder", "organ_systems": ["musculoskeletal", "endocrine"], "primary_clerkship": "pediatrics", "secondary_clerkships": ["familyMedicine"], "commonness": "uncommon", "settings": ["pediatric_clinic", "clinic"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$People with dwarfism have short stature. This means that their height is under 4' 10" as an adult. They are usually of normal intelligence. Dwarfism most often does happen in families where both parents are of average height.
More than 300 different conditions can cause dwarfism. Achondroplasia is the most common type of dwarfism. Achondroplasia is a genetic condition that affects about 1 in 15,000 to 1 in 40,000 people. It makes your arms and legs short in comparison to your head and trunk. You may also have a larger head and weak muscle tone. Other genetic conditions, kidney disease, and problems with metabolism or hormones can also cause dwarfism.
The conditions that cause dwarfism can also cause other health problems. Most of them are treatable. It is important to have regular checkups throughout your life. With proper medical care, most people with dwarfism have active lives and live as long as other people.
MeSH definition (Dwarfism): A genetic or pathological condition that is characterized by short stature and undersize. Abnormal skeletal growth usually results in an adult who is significantly below the average height.
Also called: Little person
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Adrenal Gland Disorders, Alpha-1 Antitrypsin Deficiency, Ataxia Telangiectasia, Cystic Fibrosis, Diabetes, Diabetes in Children and Teens, Diabetic Heart Disease, Endocrine Diseases, Leg Injuries and Disorders, Marfan Syndrome, Muscular Dystrophy, Osteogenesis Imperfecta, Parathyroid Disorders, Pituitary Disorders, Thyroid Diseases$rounds_kb$)
