insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$folic-acid$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1611$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Folic Acid$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$uncommon$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$obgyn$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$blood$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$endocrine$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Folic Acid", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Folate deficiency is a reachable diagnosis: dietary history, alcohol use, macrocytic anemia and low folate levels, with important pregnancy implications.", "kind": "disease", "category": "nutritional deficiency", "organ_systems": ["blood", "endocrine"], "primary_clerkship": "internalMedicine", "secondary_clerkships": ["obgyn", "familyMedicine"], "commonness": "uncommon", "settings": ["clinic", "inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Folic acid is a B vitamin. It helps the body make healthy new cells. Everyone needs folic acid. For women who may get pregnant, it is really important. Getting enough folic acid before and during pregnancy can prevent major birth defects of her baby's brain or spine.
Foods with folic acid in them include:
- Leafy green vegetables
- Fruits
- Dried beans, peas, and nuts
- Enriched breads, cereals and other grain products
If you don't get enough folic acid from the foods you eat, you can also take it as a dietary supplement.
NIH: National Institutes of Health Office of Dietary Supplements
CDC (public): About Folic Acid
About Folic Acid
Key points
- Folic acid is a type of folate (vitamin B9) that helps your body make new cells.
- All women capable of becoming pregnant should get 400 micrograms (mcg) of folic acid daily.
- Getting 400 mcg of folic acid daily can help prevent serious birth defects called neural tube defects.
Why it's important
Folic acid is a type of folate (vitamin B9) that helps your body make new cells (such as blood cells, brain cells, skin, hair, and nails). Every new cell in the body needs folate.
Folic acid is especially important during early pregnancy, because it helps the neural tube, which becomes the brain and spine, develop. It is the only type of folate shown in studies to help prevent serious birth defects called neural tube defects (NTDs). NTDs are major birth defects of a baby's brain (such as anencephaly) and spine (such as spina bifida).
NTDs happen in the first few weeks of pregnancy, often before you know you are pregnant. By the time you realize you're pregnant, it might be too late to prevent an NTD. That is why getting 400 mcg of folic acid before and during early pregnancy is important to help prevent NTDs.
Even if you're not planning to become pregnant, getting 400 mcg of folic acid daily is important. Your body uses folic acid to make new cells. Start a healthy habit today by getting 400 mcg of folic acid every day.
Start getting 400 mcg of folic acid daily today!
Terms to know
The terms folate and folic acid are often used interchangeably. However, folate is a general name for all the different types of vitamin B9. This includes food folates found in foods you eat, like leafy greens and beans, and man-made types, such as:
- Dihydrofolate (DHF)
- Tetrahydrofolate (THF)
- 5, 10-methylenetetrahydrofolate (5, 10-methylene-THF)
- 5-methyltetrahydrofolate (5-methyl-THF or 5-MTHF)
- Folic acid
Folic acid is a type of folate that is used in supplements (vitamins) and fortified foods, such as breakfast cereals. It is the only type of folate shown to help prevent NTDs.
Keep ReadingFolic Acid: Sources and Recommended Intake
Recommendations
General recommendation
If you could become pregnant, the U.S. Public Health Service recommends that you get 400 mcg of folic acid daily to help prevent NTDs.1This recommendation is also supported by the Institute of Medicine and the US Preventive Services Task Force.
Planning a pregnancy after having a baby with an NTD
If you had a past pregnancy with an NTD and you're planning to become pregnant again, 
MeSH definition (Folic Acid Deficiency): A nutritional condition produced by a deficiency of FOLIC ACID in the diet. Many plant and animal tissues contain folic acid, abundant in green leafy vegetables, yeast, liver, and mushrooms but destroyed by long-term cooking. Alcohol interferes with its intermediate metabolism and absorption. Folic acid deficiency may develop in long-term anticonvulsant therapy or with use of oral contraceptives. This deficiency causes anemia, macrocytic anemia, and megaloblastic anemia. It is indistinguishable from vitamin B 12 deficiency in peripheral blood and bone marrow findings, but the neurologic lesions seen in B 12 deficiency do not occur. (Merck Manual, 16th ed)
MeSH definition (Folic Acid): A member of the vitamin B family that stimulates the hematopoietic system. It is present in the liver and kidney and is found in mushrooms, spinach, yeast, green leaves, and grasses (POACEAE). Folic acid is used in the treatment and prevention of folate deficiencies and megaloblastic anemia.
Also called: Folacin, Folate, Pteroylglutamic acid, Vitamin B9$rounds_kb$)
