insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$foreign-bodies$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-5607$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Foreign Bodies$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$uncommon$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$surgery$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$gastrointestinal$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Foreign Bodies", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "Mechanistic category rather than a diagnosis; presentation varies entirely by site and object.", "kind": "other", "category": "injury (umbrella)", "organ_systems": ["gastrointestinal"], "primary_clerkship": "emergency", "secondary_clerkships": ["surgery", "pediatrics"], "commonness": "uncommon", "settings": ["emergency"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$If you've ever gotten a splinter or had sand in your eye, you've had experience with a foreign body. A foreign body is something that is stuck inside you but isn't supposed to be there. You may inhale or swallow a foreign body, or you may get one from an injury to almost any part of your body. Foreign bodies are more common in small children, who sometimes stick things in their mouths, ears, and noses.
Some foreign bodies, like a small splinter, do not cause serious harm. Inhaled or swallowed foreign bodies may cause choking or bowel obstruction and may require medical care.
MeSH definition (Foreign Bodies): Inanimate objects that become enclosed in the body.
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Animal Bites, Arm Injuries and Disorders, Back Injuries, Barotrauma, Burns, Chest Injuries and Disorders, Dislocations, Drowning, Electrical Injuries, Fractures, Frostbite, Hand Injuries and Disorders, Heat Illness, Hip Injuries and Disorders, Leg Injuries and Disorders$rounds_kb$)
