insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$hearing-disorders-and-deafness$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-204$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Hearing Disorders and Deafness$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$nervous$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Hearing Disorders and Deafness", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "Broad category of hearing impairment rather than a specific diagnosis; presentations and workups vary widely.", "kind": "other", "category": "otologic category", "organ_systems": ["nervous"], "primary_clerkship": "familyMedicine", "secondary_clerkships": [], "commonness": "common", "settings": ["clinic"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$It's frustrating to be unable to hear well enough to enjoy talking with friends or family. Hearing disorders make it hard, but not impossible, to hear. They can often be helped. Deafness can keep you from hearing sound at all.
What causes hearing loss? Some possibilities are:
- Heredity
- Diseases such as ear infections and meningitis
- Trauma
- Certain medicines
- Long-term exposure to loud noise
- Aging
There are two main types of hearing loss. One happens when your inner ear or auditory nerve is damaged. This type is usually permanent. The other kind happens when sound waves cannot reach your inner ear. Earwax buildup, fluid, or a punctured eardrum can cause it. Treatment or surgery can often reverse this kind of hearing loss.
Untreated, hearing problems can get worse. If you have trouble hearing, you can get help. Possible treatments include hearing aids, cochlear implants, special training, certain medicines, and surgery.
NIH: National Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders
MeSH definition (Hearing Disorders): Conditions that impair the transmission of auditory impulses and information from the level of the ear to the temporal cortices, including the sensorineural pathways.
MeSH definition (Deafness): A general term for the complete loss of the ability to hear from both ears.
Also called: Hearing loss, Presbycusis
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Dizziness and Vertigo, Hearing Problems in Children, Taste and Smell Disorders, Vision Impairment and Blindness$rounds_kb$)
