insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$herniated-disk$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-284$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Herniated Disk$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$musculoskeletal$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$nervous$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Herniated Disk", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Radicular back and leg pain with dermatomal exam findings; diagnosis by history, exam and imaging, with a clear differential of back pain.", "kind": "disease", "category": "spinal disorder", "organ_systems": ["musculoskeletal", "nervous"], "primary_clerkship": "internalMedicine", "secondary_clerkships": ["familyMedicine", "neurology", "emergency"], "commonness": "common", "settings": ["clinic", "emergency"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Your backbone, or spine, is made up of 26 bones called vertebrae. In between them are soft disks filled with a jelly-like substance. These disks cushion the vertebrae and keep them in place. As you age, the disks break down or degenerate. As they do, they lose their cushioning ability. This can lead to pain if the back is stressed.
A herniated disk is a disk that ruptures. This allows the jelly-like center of the disk to leak, irritating the nearby nerves. This can cause sciatica or back pain.
Your doctor will diagnose a herniated disk with a physical exam and, sometimes, imaging tests. With treatment, most people recover. Treatments include rest, pain and anti-inflammatory medicines, physical therapy, and sometimes surgery.
NIH: National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases
MeSH definition (Intervertebral Disc Displacement): An INTERVERTEBRAL DISC in which the NUCLEUS PULPOSUS has protruded through surrounding ANNULUS FIBROSUS. This occurs most frequently in the lower lumbar region.
Also called: Bulging disk, Compressed disk, Herniated intervertebral disk, Herniated nucleus pulposus, Prolapsed disk, Ruptured disk, Slipped disk
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Pelvic Floor Disorders, Spinal Stenosis$rounds_kb$)
