insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$huntington-s-disease$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1218$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Huntington's Disease$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$rare$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$psychiatry$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$nervous$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$behavioral$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Huntington's Disease", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disease with chorea, psychiatric and cognitive features; diagnosis reachable by family history, exam and genetic testing.", "kind": "disease", "category": "neurodegenerative / movement disorder", "organ_systems": ["nervous", "behavioral"], "primary_clerkship": "neurology", "secondary_clerkships": ["psychiatry", "internalMedicine", "familyMedicine"], "commonness": "rare", "settings": ["clinic", "inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Huntington's disease (HD) is an inherited disease that causes certain nerve cells in the brain to waste away. People are born with the defective gene, but symptoms usually don't appear until middle age. Early symptoms of HD may include uncontrolled movements, clumsiness, and balance problems. Later, HD can take away the ability to walk, talk, and swallow. Some people stop recognizing family members. Others are aware of their environment and are able to express emotions.
If one of your parents has Huntington's disease, you have a 50% chance of getting it. A blood test can tell you if have the HD gene and will develop the disease. Genetic counseling can help you weigh the risks and benefits of taking the test.
There is no cure. Medicines can help manage some of the symptoms, but cannot slow down or stop the disease.
NIH: National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke
MeSH definition (Huntington Disease): A familial disorder inherited as an autosomal dominant trait and characterized by the onset of progressive CHOREA and DEMENTIA in the fourth or fifth decade of life. Common initial manifestations include paranoia; poor impulse control; DEPRESSION; HALLUCINATIONS; and DELUSIONS. Eventually intellectual impairment; loss of fine motor control; ATHETOSIS; and diffuse chorea involving axial and limb musculature develops, leading to a vegetative state within 10-15 years of disease onset. The juvenile variant has a more fulminant course including SEIZURES; ATAXIA; dementia; and chorea. (From Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, pp1060-4)
Also called: HD, Huntington's chorea
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Alzheimer's Caregivers, Alzheimer's Disease, Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease, Leukodystrophies, Lewy Body Dementia, Mild Cognitive Impairment, Neurofibromatosis, Progressive Supranuclear Palsy, Tourette Syndrome, Tuberous Sclerosis, Wilson Disease$rounds_kb$)
