insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$osteonecrosis$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1647$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Osteonecrosis$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$uncommon$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$surgery$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$surgery$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$musculoskeletal$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Osteonecrosis", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Presents with focal joint pain and risk factors (steroids, alcohol, trauma); diagnosis reachable by history, exam and MRI.", "kind": "disease", "category": "bone ischemia", "organ_systems": ["musculoskeletal"], "primary_clerkship": "surgery", "secondary_clerkships": ["internalMedicine", "familyMedicine", "emergency"], "commonness": "uncommon", "settings": ["clinic", "inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Osteonecrosis is a disease caused by reduced blood flow to bones in the joints. In people with healthy bones, new bone is always replacing old bone. In osteonecrosis, the lack of blood causes the bone to break down faster than the body can make enough new bone. The bone starts to die and may break down.
You can have osteonecrosis in one or several bones. It is most common in the upper leg. Other common sites are your upper arm and your knees, shoulders and ankles. The disease can affect men and women of any age, but it usually strikes in your thirties, forties or fifties.
At first, you might not have any symptoms. As the disease gets worse, you will probably have joint pain that becomes more severe. You may not be able to bend or move the affected joint very well.
No one is sure what causes the disease. Risk factors include :
- Long-term steroid treatment
- Alcohol misuse
- Joint injuries
- Having certain diseases, including arthritis and cancer
Doctors use imaging tests and other tests to diagnose osteonecrosis. Treatments include medicines, using crutches, limiting activities that put weight on the affected joints, electrical stimulation and surgery.
NIH: National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases
MeSH definition (Osteonecrosis): Death of a bone or part of a bone, either atraumatic or posttraumatic.
Also called: Aseptic necrosis, Avascular necrosis, Ischemic necrosis
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Bone Cancer, Bone Infections, Gangrene, Paget's Disease of Bone, Spine Injuries and Disorders, Tailbone Disorders$rounds_kb$)
