insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$paralysis$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-611$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Paralysis$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$uncommon$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$neurology$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$nervous$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Paralysis", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "A broad symptom/sign category rather than a single diagnosis; the underlying cause (stroke, spinal cord injury, Guillain-Barre, etc.) is what a student would work up.", "kind": "symptom", "category": "motor deficit", "organ_systems": ["nervous"], "primary_clerkship": "neurology", "secondary_clerkships": ["emergency", "internalMedicine"], "commonness": "uncommon", "settings": ["emergency", "inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Paralysis is the loss of muscle function in part of your body. It happens when something goes wrong with the way messages pass between your brain and muscles. Paralysis can be complete or partial. It can occur on one or both sides of your body. It can also occur in just one area, or it can be widespread. Paralysis of the lower half of your body, including both legs, is called paraplegia. Paralysis of the arms and legs is quadriplegia.
Most paralysis is due to strokes or injuries such as spinal cord injury or a broken neck. Other causes of paralysis include:
- Nerve diseases such as amyotrophic lateral sclerosis
- Autoimmune diseases such as Guillain-Barre syndrome
- Bell's palsy, which affects muscles in the face
Polio used to be a cause of paralysis, but polio no longer occurs in the U.S.
MeSH definition (Paralysis): A general term most often used to describe severe or complete loss of muscle strength due to motor system disease from the level of the cerebral cortex to the muscle fiber. This term may also occasionally refer to a loss of sensory function. (From Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, p45)
Also called: Hemiplegia, Palsy, Paraplegia, Quadriplegia
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Dizziness and Vertigo, Pain, Seizures, Sleep Disorders, Voice Disorders, Walking Problems$rounds_kb$)
