insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$rare-diseases$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-519$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Rare Diseases$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$rare$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$blood$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Rare Diseases", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "A broad category of many unrelated conditions, not a single diagnosis with a patient presentation.", "kind": "other", "category": "category", "organ_systems": ["blood"], "primary_clerkship": "internalMedicine", "secondary_clerkships": [], "commonness": "rare", "settings": ["clinic"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$A rare disease is one that affects fewer than 200,000 people in the United States. There are nearly 7,000 rare diseases. More than 25 million Americans have one.
Rare diseases:
- May involve chronic illness, disability, and often premature death
- Often have no treatment or not very effective treatment
- Are frequently not diagnosed correctly
- Are often very complex
- Are often caused by changes in genes
It can be hard to find a specialist who knows how to treat your rare disease. Disease advocacy groups, rare disease organizations, and genetics clinics may help you to find one.
NIH: National Institutes of Health
MeSH definition (Rare Diseases): A large group of diseases which are characterized by a low prevalence in the population. They frequently are associated with problems in diagnosis and treatment.
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Coping with Chronic Illness, Infectious Diseases, Winter Weather Emergencies$rounds_kb$)
