insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$sun-exposure$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-1419$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Sun Exposure$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$very_common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$musculoskeletal$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Sun Exposure", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "A prevention/exposure topic rather than a diagnosis reachable by interview and workup; sunburn is a symptom-level finding, not a case-defining illness.", "kind": "other", "category": "environmental exposure", "organ_systems": ["musculoskeletal"], "primary_clerkship": "familyMedicine", "secondary_clerkships": ["emergency", "pediatrics"], "commonness": "very_common", "settings": ["clinic", "emergency"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Ultraviolet (UV) rays are an invisible form of radiation. They can pass through your skin and damage your skin cells. Sunburns are a sign of skin damage. Suntans aren't healthy, either. They appear after the sun's rays have already killed some cells and damaged others. UV rays can cause skin damage during any season or at any temperature. They can also cause eye problems, wrinkles, skin spots, and skin cancer.
To protect yourself :
- Stay out of the sun when it is strongest (between 10 a.m. and 2 p.m.)
- Use sunscreen with an SPF of 15 or higher
- Wear protective clothing
- Wear wraparound sunglasses that provide 100% UV ray protection
- Avoid sunlamps and tanning beds
Check your skin regularly for changes in the size, shape, color, or feel of birthmarks, moles, and spots. Such changes are a sign of skin cancer.
Food and Drug Administration
CDC (public): NIOSH Fast Facts: Protecting Yourself from Sun Exposure
Content provided and maintained by the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Please see our system usage guidelines and disclaimer.
MeSH definition (Sunscreening Agents): Chemical or physical agents that protect the skin from sunburn and erythema by absorbing or blocking ultraviolet radiation.
MeSH definition (Sunbathing): Exposing oneself to SUNLIGHT or ULTRAVIOLET RAYS.
MeSH definition (Sunburn): An injury to the skin causing erythema, tenderness, and sometimes blistering and resulting from excessive exposure to the sun. The reaction is produced by the ultraviolet radiation in sunlight.
Also called: Sunburn
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Electrical Injuries, Inhalation Injuries$rounds_kb$)
