insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$tumors-and-pregnancy$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-5534$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Tumors and Pregnancy$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$rare$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$obgyn$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$obgyn$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$reproductive$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Tumors and Pregnancy", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "A co-occurrence category rather than a single diagnosis; management depends entirely on the specific cancer and gestational age.", "kind": "other", "category": "pregnancy with malignancy", "organ_systems": ["reproductive"], "primary_clerkship": "obgyn", "secondary_clerkships": ["internalMedicine"], "commonness": "rare", "settings": ["inpatient", "clinic"], "is_cancer": true}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Tumors during pregnancy are rare, but they can happen. Tumors can be either benign (not cancer) or malignant (cancer). The most common cancers in pregnancy include breast cancer, thyroid cancer, cervical cancer, lymphoma, and melanoma. Cancer itself rarely harms the fetus, and some cancer treatments are safe during pregnancy. You and your health care provider will work together to find the best treatment. Your choices will depend on how far along the pregnancy is, as well as the type, size, and stage of your cancer.
Another type of tumor that women can get is called a gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD). It happens when a fertilized egg doesn't become a fetus. GTD is not always easy to find. It is usually benign, but some types can be malignant. The most common type of GTD is a molar pregnancy. In its early stages, it may look like a normal pregnancy. You should see your provider if you have vaginal bleeding (not menstrual bleeding).
Treatment depends on the type of tumor, whether it has spread to other places, and your overall health.
MeSH definition (Pregnancy Complications, Neoplastic): The co-occurrence of pregnancy and NEOPLASMS. The neoplastic disease may precede or follow FERTILIZATION.
MeSH definition (Pregnancy Complications): Conditions or pathological processes associated with pregnancy. They can occur during or after pregnancy, and range from minor discomforts to serious diseases that require medical interventions. They include diseases in pregnant females, and pregnancies in females with diseases.
MeSH definition (Neoplasms): New abnormal growth of tissue. Malignant neoplasms show a greater degree of anaplasia and have the properties of invasion and metastasis, compared to benign neoplasms.
MeSH definition (Pregnancy): The status during which female mammals carry their developing young (EMBRYOS or FETUSES) in utero before birth, beginning from FERTILIZATION to BIRTH.
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Benign Tumors, Cancer, Cancer Alternative Therapies, Cancer Chemotherapy, Cancer Immunotherapy, Cancer in Children, Cancer--Living with Cancer, Childbirth Problems, Diabetes and Pregnancy, Ectopic Pregnancy, Fetal Health and Development, HIV and Pregnancy, Health Problems in Pregnancy, High Blood Pressure in Pregnancy, Infections and Pregnancy$rounds_kb$)
