insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$urinary-tract-infections$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-448$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Urinary Tract Infections$rounds_kb$, true, $rounds_kb$very_common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$internalMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$familyMedicine$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$pediatrics$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$emergency$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$obgyn$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$renal$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Urinary Tract Infections", "case_suitable": true, "reason": "Very common infection with classic dysuria/frequency presentation, straightforward diagnosis by history, exam and urinalysis; important to distinguish cystitis from pyelonephritis.", "kind": "disease", "category": "infection", "organ_systems": ["renal"], "primary_clerkship": "internalMedicine", "secondary_clerkships": ["familyMedicine", "pediatrics", "emergency", "obgyn"], "commonness": "very_common", "settings": ["clinic", "emergency", "inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$The urinary system is the body's drainage system for removing wastes and extra water. It includes two kidneys, two ureters, a bladder, and a urethra. Urinary tract infections (UTIs) are the second most common type of infection in the body.
You may have a UTI if you notice:
- Pain or burning when you urinate
- Fever, tiredness, or shakiness
- An urge to urinate often
- Pressure in your lower belly
- Urine that smells bad or looks cloudy or reddish
- Pain in your back or side below the ribs
People of any age or sex can get UTIs. But about four times as many women get UTIs as men. You're also at higher risk if you have diabetes, need a tube to drain your bladder, or have a spinal cord injury.
If you think you have a UTI it is important to see your doctor. Your doctor can tell if you have a UTI with a urine test. Treatment is with antibiotics.
NIH: National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases
CDC (clinician): Strategize Initiatives You Can Incorporate into Your Program
Strategize Initiatives You Can Incorporate into Your Program
Urine Culture Stewardship in Hospitalized Patients
What to know
- Prior to developing and employing specific strategies, it may be useful to define facility specific criteria or protocols for the following: indication for urine cultures and urine sample collection.
- These can be used as the basis for a number of the strategies described below.
Use of urine cultures in patients with indwelling urinary catheters
Here are some examples of appropriate and inappropriate uses of urine cultures that you can consider when drafting your list of facility-approved indications, other indications might exist at your facility:
Examples of appropriate & inappropriate use
These examples are not meant to replace the clinical judgement of an individual treating provider.
Appropriate uses of urine cultures
- Presence of symptoms suggestive of a urinary tract infection (UTI). For example;
- Flank pain or costovertebral angle tenderness,
- acute hematuria,
- new pelvic discomfort
- New onset or worsening sepsis without evidence of another source on history, physical examination, or laboratory testing
- Fever or altered mental status without evidence of another source on history, physical examination, or laboratory testing
- In spinal cord injury patients: increased spasticity, autonomic dysreflexia, sense of unease
Inappropriate uses of urine cultures
- Odorous, cloudy, or discolored urine in the absence of other localizing signs/symptoms
- Reflex urine cultures based on urinalysis results, such as pyuria, in the absence of other indications (Absence of pyuria suggests diagnosis other than CAUTI)
- Urine culture to document response to therapy unless symptoms are fail to resolve
Diagnosing CAUTI vs. CA-ASB
It is important to distinguish between catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI) and catheter-associated asymptomatic bacteriuria (CA-ASB) to determine if treatment with antibiotics is required.
Some points to consider and help differentiate CAUTI from CA-ASB:
Differences between CAUTI and CA-ASB on three characteristicsCharacteristicsCAUTICA-ASBSigns/symptomsSigns or symptoms compatible with UTI and no other identified source of infectionNo signs or symptoms compatible with UTIMicrobiology≥103 colony-forming units (cfu)/ml of ≥1 bacterial species in a single catheter urine specimen or in a midstream voided urine specimen from a patient whose catheter has been removed within the previous 48 hours≥105 cfu
MeSH definition (Urinary Tract Infections): Inflammatory responses of the epithelium of the URINARY TRACT to microbial invasions. They are often bacterial infections with associated BACTERIURIA and PYURIA.
Also called: UTI
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Animal Diseases and Your Health, Bladder Diseases, Bone Infections, Eye Infections, Infections and Pregnancy, Infectious Arthritis, Infectious Diseases, Kidney Diseases, Parasitic Diseases, Sepsis, Sexually Transmitted Infections, Skin Infections, Ureteral Disorders, Urethral Disorders, Urine and Urination$rounds_kb$)
