insert into private.conditions (slug, condition_id, name, case_suitable, commonness, primary_clerkship, clerkships, organ_systems, triage, background) values ($rounds_kb$weight-loss-surgery$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$mplus-3302$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$Weight Loss Surgery$rounds_kb$, false, $rounds_kb$common$rounds_kb$, $rounds_kb$surgery$rounds_kb$, array[$rounds_kb$surgery$rounds_kb$]::text[], array[$rounds_kb$gastrointestinal$rounds_kb$]::text[], $rounds_kb${"name": "Weight Loss Surgery", "case_suitable": false, "reason": "A treatment topic rather than a diagnosis reachable by interview and workup.", "kind": "other", "category": "treatment", "organ_systems": ["gastrointestinal"], "primary_clerkship": "surgery", "secondary_clerkships": [], "commonness": "common", "settings": ["inpatient"], "is_cancer": false}$rounds_kb$::jsonb, $rounds_kb$Weight loss surgery helps people with extreme obesity to lose weight. It may be an option if you cannot lose weight through diet and exercise or have serious health problems caused by obesity.
There are different types of weight loss surgery. They often limit the amount of food you can take in. Some types of surgery also affect how you digest food and absorb nutrients. All types have risks and complications, such as infections, hernias, and blood clots.
Many people who have the surgery lose weight quickly, but regain some weight later on. If you follow diet and exercise recommendations, you can keep most of the weight off. You will also need medical follow-up for the rest of your life.
NIH: National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases
MeSH definition (Bariatric Surgery): Surgical procedures aimed at affecting metabolism and producing major WEIGHT REDUCTION in patients with MORBID OBESITY.
MeSH definition (Obesity): A status with BODY WEIGHT that is grossly above the recommended standards, usually due to accumulation of excess FATS in the body. The standards may vary with age, sex, genetic or cultural background. In the BODY MASS INDEX, a BMI greater than 30.0 kg/m2 is considered obese, and a BMI greater than 40.0 kg/m2 is considered morbidly obese (MORBID OBESITY).
MeSH definition (Obesity, Morbid): The condition of weighing two, three, or more times the ideal weight, so called because it is associated with many serious and life-threatening disorders. In the BODY MASS INDEX, morbid obesity is defined as having a BMI greater than 40.0 kg/m2.
Also called: Bariatric surgery, Bypass surgery, Gastric banding, Obesity surgery
Related conditions (candidate look-alikes): Obesity, Obesity in Children, Prader-Willi Syndrome$rounds_kb$)
